/// An iterator a `parallel for` can split between threads (spec §22.3).
///
/// `parallel for &mut e in enemies` loops over what `for` would (§10.4), here the list's
/// `iter_mut()`, and needs that iterator to implement this trait: the loop asks how many
/// passes there are, cuts them into one range per thread, and walks each range with an
/// iterator of its own from `part`.
///
/// The promise an implementation makes is that two parts over ranges that do not overlap hand
/// out no element in common, so each pass is given an element no other pass is given. An
/// iterator that hands out `&mut` keeps it inside `unsafe`, as a container keeps its `kills`.
///
/// ```dz
/// parallel for &mut e in enemies {
///     e.think()
/// }
/// ```
pub trait ParallelIterate {
    /// How many passes are left, from where the iterator stands.
    fn passes(&self) -> usize

    /// The passes from `start` to `end`, counted from where the iterator stands, as an
    /// iterator of their own: `start <= end <= passes()`.
    fn part(&self, start: usize, end: usize) -> Self
}
